WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.400
Groups, broadcast lists, channels and communities.

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.490
This is the video for module three of WhatsApp for Communication and Business.

3
00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:17.670
WhatsApp gives you four ways to reach many people at once. Each one suits a different job, and choosing well saves everyone time.

4
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.230
You will learn to set up a group like a careful admin, so it stays on purpose.

5
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.730
You will learn how scammers use groups to ask for money, and how to stop them.

6
00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.460
And you will tame the buzzing, because many of us belong to 30 or more groups.

7
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:47.460
A group is for conversation. Everyone sees and replies to every message. It can hold up to 1,024 members, good for teams, cooperatives and church departments.

8
00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:58.670
A broadcast list sends the same message as a private copy to up to 256 contacts. They do not see each other, and replies come only to you.

9
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.660
But only people who saved your number receive it, so always ask new customers to save your number.

10
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:16.790
A channel, in the Updates tab, is a one-way public tool. Followers can react and vote in polls, but cannot reply, and phone numbers stay hidden on both sides.

11
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:24.140
A community links related groups, like branches, with one announcement group that reaches every member.

12
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:36.890
Mama Funke chairs a 60-member women's thrift cooperative, an ajo, in Ibadan. Their old group was full of forwarded videos, and members missed contribution reminders.

13
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:43.760
Step one. She creates a new group with a clear name, Bodija Women Ajo, and the cooperative's logo.

14
00:01:45.010 --> 00:01:54.320
Step two. The description says: for contributions, meetings and loans only. No forwards, no adverts. Contributions due every Friday.

15
00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:00.200
Step three. She sets Edit group settings to admins only, to stop pranks.

16
00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:11.260
Step four. She turns on Approve new members, so strangers cannot slip in through the link. If a link gets into the wrong hands, Reset link stops it working.

17
00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:18.200
Step five. She makes the treasurer and secretary admins, and pins the payment account details.

18
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:30.100
Scammers copy an admin's name and photo, then privately ask members to pay into a new account. So she pins this warning, and members confirm by phone with the treasurer before paying.

19
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:42.890
Uche sells foodstuff in bulk in Onitsha to about 400 regular buyers, and prices change often. One big group would expose every buyer's number and invite arguments.

20
00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:53.310
Instead she makes two broadcast lists of 200 contacts each, under the 256 limit. She asks every buyer to save her number.

21
00:02:53.660 --> 00:03:02.790
Every Monday she sends this week's prices, marked approximate, call to confirm. Replies arrive privately, so she takes orders one by one.

22
00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:19.850
Good group manners are simple. Stay on the group's purpose, reply to the exact message, and avoid posting between about 10pm and 6am except in emergencies. Never forward unverified health, security or money alerts.

23
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:29.360
Type the at sign and a name to mention someone. It reaches them even if they muted the group, so use it only when you truly need them.

24
00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:44.490
To decide something, create a poll. Precious, secretary of a 90-member alumni group in Calabar, offered three dates. After 48 hours, 52 votes chose one date, with almost no extra messages.

25
00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:55.450
Press and hold a busy group, tap mute, and choose 8 hours, 1 week or Always. Give your most important group its own tone, so you know it by sound.

26
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:04.530
Use the Unread and Groups filters to catch up quickly. And once a month, exit groups that no longer serve you, with a short polite thank-you.

27
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.340
To remember. Use a group to talk and a broadcast list for known contacts.

28
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.360
Use a channel for the public and a community for many branches.

29
00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:23.610
Give every group a clear name, rules and admins-only settings. Pin the payment warning. And use polls for decisions and mute for noise.

30
00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:34.260
Now practise. Create a small group with two or three trusted people and three rules. Build a broadcast list, mute five busy groups, and run a poll.

31
00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:36.770
Then continue with the lessons below.
